    "events": [
      {
        "met_days": 0,
        "type": "launch",
        "label": "Launch — Atlas V 401",
        "description": "Cape Canaveral SLC-41, 2021-10-16. Lucy is the first mission to study the Jupiter Trojans — primordial asteroids leading and trailing Jupiter at its L4 and L5 Lagrange points by 60°. The trojans are believed to be remnants of the outer protoplanetary disc, captured into the Lagrange points during early solar system migration. Lucy's 12-year primary mission visits eight asteroids on separate solar orbits — two main-belt (Dinkinesh, Donaldjohanson) and six Trojans, plus their satellites — a record for a single planetary science mission."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 0.005,
        "type": "tli_or_tmi",
        "label": "Trans-trojan injection",
        "description": "Centaur upper stage delivers Lucy onto a complex multi-encounter trajectory using three Earth gravity assists (2022, 2024, 2030) to reach both Trojan swarms."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 365,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Earth gravity assist 1",
        "description": "2022-10-16. First EGA accelerates Lucy onto a higher heliocentric orbit, reaching beyond Mars."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 746,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Dinkinesh — first asteroid flyby",
        "description": "2023-11-01. Closest approach to 152830 Dinkinesh (~790 m main-belt asteroid). Lucy's first-ever asteroid encounter. Imaging revealed the contact-binary satellite Selam — a previously unknown moon — a headline discovery independent of the primary Trojan mission."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 1154,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Earth gravity assist 2",
        "description": "2024-12-13 (per redesigned profile)."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 1282,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Donaldjohanson (main-belt bonus)",
        "description": "2025-04-20. Closest approach 960 km at 13.4 km/s past asteroid 52246 Donaldjohanson — a 4-km main-belt asteroid named for the discoverer of the Lucy hominid fossil. Bonus target added late in mission design to test instruments before the Trojan encounters."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2126,
        "type": "arrival",
        "label": "Eurybates approach (L4 Trojan)",
        "description": "2027-08-12. Lucy arrives at the L4 Trojan cloud."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2126.001,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Eurybates + Queta",
        "description": "Closest approach 1,000 km at 5.7 km/s past 3548 Eurybates, the largest member of the L4 Eurybates family — a 64-km C-type fragment of a larger parent body. Queta is Eurybates's 1-km moon, discovered by Hubble in 2018. Lucy's first Trojan encounter."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2160,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Polymele",
        "description": "2027-09-15. 21-km P-type Trojan; Hubble follow-up revealed a satellite that doesn't yet have a name."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2376,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Leucus",
        "description": "2028-04-18. 35-km D-type Trojan, slow rotator (446-hour period)."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2583,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Orus",
        "description": "2028-11-11. 51-km D-type Trojan."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 3358,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Earth gravity assist 3",
        "description": "2030-12-26. Final EGA accelerates Lucy outbound to the trailing L5 cloud."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 4155,
        "type": "edl_or_oi",
        "label": "Patroclus-Menoetius binary (L5)",
        "description": "2033-03-02. The mission climax: encounter with binary Trojan Patroclus-Menoetius. Patroclus (113 km) + Menoetius (104 km) are a nearly-equal-mass binary on the L5 side — the largest Trojan binary known. Closest approach 1,000 km at 8.4 km/s. After 12 years of cruise, Lucy will have visited 11 bodies across 8 asteroids and their satellites — a record number of objects on separate heliocentric orbits for any planetary science mission."
      }
    ],
